# backup-local bootstrap

restic backup of every local-path PVC to the munin external drive. This is
the local half of the backup story; `helm/velero` owns the cloud half. See
`docs/adr/0005-backup-restore.md`.

## 1. Make sure the drive is mounted

`/run/media/mugiwara/munin` is a udisks automount (`/dev/sdb1`, exFAT, 1.9T).
The CronJob mounts it with `hostPath: type: Directory` and additionally
checks `mountpoint -q` before writing, so if the drive is detached the job
fails loudly instead of backing up to the OS disk. Nothing to do here as
long as the drive is attached.

## 2. Create the repository password Secret (never committed)

restic encrypts the repository with this password. **Losing it makes every
snapshot on the drive permanently unreadable** — store it in the same vault
as the `copy_secrets.sh` export, alongside the Sealed Secrets signing key.

```
kubectl create namespace homelab-backup-local
kubectl -n homelab-backup-local create secret generic backup-local-secrets \
  --from-literal=RESTIC_PASSWORD='<pick-a-strong-passphrase>'
```

This is created out-of-band rather than sealed for the same reason as
Velero's credentials: it is the key that unlocks the backups, so it must
exist in a vault independently of anything stored in this cluster or repo.
A SealedSecret would be decryptable only by the controller whose own key
lives in the backups it protects.

## 3. Create the watchdog Secret (never committed)

The `backup-watchdog` CronJob pings a healthchecks.io check daily, but only
while the newest *completed* Velero backup is under 8 days old. A failed,
partially-failed or missing backup means no ping, and healthchecks.io alerts
on the missed check-in — which also catches an entirely dead cluster.

Create a check at <https://healthchecks.io> with period 1 day / grace 36
hours, then:

```
kubectl -n homelab-backup-local create secret generic backup-watchdog-secrets \
  --from-literal=HEALTHCHECK_URL='https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>'
```

Set `watchdog.enabled: false` in `values.yaml` if you would rather not run it
than run it un-configured — the CronJob will fail every day without the
Secret, which is noisy but not harmful.

## 4. First run

The CronJob fires Sundays 04:00. Trigger one immediately to initialise the
repository and confirm it works end to end:

```
kubectl -n homelab-backup-local create job --from=cronjob/backup-local \
  manual-$(date +%Y%m%d)
kubectl -n homelab-backup-local logs -f job/manual-$(date +%Y%m%d)
```

Expect `No restic repository at /drive/restic — initialising.` on the first
run, then a snapshot summary.

## Restoring

The restic repository lives at `/run/media/mugiwara/munin/restic`. Restore
from any machine with the drive and the password — no cluster required,
which is the point of keeping this path independent of Velero:

```
export RESTIC_REPOSITORY=/run/media/mugiwara/munin/restic
export RESTIC_PASSWORD='<passphrase>'

restic snapshots                          # find the snapshot
restic restore <snapshot-id> --target /tmp/restore

# Restore one stack's PVC only:
restic restore <snapshot-id> --target /tmp/restore \
  --include '/pvcs/pvc-*_homelab-immich_*'
```

Paths inside a snapshot are `/pvcs/<pvc-uid>_<namespace>_<pvc-name>`, mirroring
local-path's directory naming under `/var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage`. Copy the
restored directory back over the live PVC directory with the workload scaled
to zero.

## Scope

Everything under `/var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage` — i.e. every PVC on the
cluster, since local-path provisions all of them under that single root.
That includes the Postgres/SQLite dumps written by the Velero pre-backup
hooks (see `helm/immich`, `helm/atuin`, `helm/yggdrasil`, `helm/nextcloud`),
so this path gets consistent logical database dumps too.

Not included: the `arr`/`jellyfin` media tree, which is a hostPath, not a
PVC — a deliberate exclusion, see ADR 0005.
